# Discrete-time UNITARY EVOLUTION, the Schrodinger picture made checkable by the kernel. A quantum state does not jump.
# It turns. Between two beats the state at beat n+1 is the unitary step operator U applied to the state at beat n, and
# running for n beats is U applied n times. This is the discrete form of Stone's theorem: a one-parameter group of
# unitaries U(t) generated by the Hamiltonian H through i d/dt |psi> = H |psi>, here with the continuous parameter
# replaced by the integer beat count. The model carries three pieces that each capture one face of the equation.
#
# First the PHASE GROUP, the clock of four phases (the powers of i). The unitary step is a single tick of this clock, a
# rotation by one quarter turn, and evolving for n beats ticks the clock n times. The kernel proves the GROUP LAW of
# time evolution, U^(m+n) = U^m then U^n, by induction on the beat count: evolution composes additively in time, the
# one-parameter group property. It also proves the step is invertible (untick undoes tick) and that four beats return to
# the start (the clock closes), the discrete unitarity of a pure phase.
#
# Second the REGISTER, a finite line of basis kets carrying occupation. The unitary step here is a cyclic shift, a
# permutation of the kets, and the NORM is the total occupation summed over the line. A permutation moves occupation
# around but never creates or destroys it, so the kernel proves the step preserves the norm, and hence by induction that
# every beat of evolution preserves it: probability is conserved, the unitarity that keeps the total at one for all time.
#
# Third the STATIONARY STATE. An eigenstate of the Hamiltonian evolves by an overall phase only, e^{-iEt}, so its
# probability density does not move. The kernel proves that a state concentrated on a single ket evolves under the phase
# tick with its occupation pattern unchanged, the time-invariant density of an energy eigenstate.
#
# Fourth the CANONICAL COMMUTATION RELATION [x, p] = i. Position and momentum do not commute. On the discrete register,
# position multiplies a ket by its coordinate and momentum shifts it, and applying them in the two orders disagrees, so
# the kernel refutes commutativity by computation: the commutator acting on a state is nonzero, the +i that makes the
# uncertainty principle.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both

# A beat count, the discrete time parameter. None is the present, each `more` is one beat later. This is the integer t in
# U(t), built as a Peano natural so evolution can recurse on it and laws can be proved by induction.
form beat
  case none
  case more
    link prior, like beat

# Adding beat counts, ordinary Peano addition on the time parameter. m + (n+1) = (m+n)+1. The group law of evolution is
# stated against this sum: evolving for m+n beats equals evolving for m then for n.
task add-beat
  take m, like beat
  take n, like beat
  like beat
  fork case, read n
    case none
      send back
        read m
    case more
      link q
      send back
        make more
          bind prior
            call add-beat
              read m
              read q

# --- THE PHASE GROUP: the clock of four phases, the powers of i. ---

# The four phases a pure state can carry, the powers of i: 1, i, -1, -i. A unitary phase evolution lives on this clock.
# The Schrodinger phase e^{-iEt} of an energy eigenstate, sampled at integer beats, walks exactly these four values.
form phase
  case one
  case eye
  case neg
  case neg-eye

# THE UNITARY STEP on a phase, one tick of the clock: a quarter turn, multiply by i. one to eye to neg to neg-eye and
# back to one. This is U for a pure phase, the discrete generator of the one-parameter group.
task tick
  take p, like phase
  like phase
  fork case, read p
    case one
      send back
        make eye
    case eye
      send back
        make neg
    case neg
      send back
        make neg-eye
    case neg-eye
      send back
        make one

# THE INVERSE STEP, untick, a quarter turn the other way (multiply by -i). It runs the evolution backward, the U-inverse
# that exists because the step is unitary.
task untick
  take p, like phase
  like phase
  fork case, read p
    case one
      send back
        make neg-eye
    case eye
      send back
        make one
    case neg
      send back
        make eye
    case neg-eye
      send back
        make neg

# EVOLVING a phase for n beats: apply the unitary step n times. evolve p (n+1) = tick (evolve p n). This is U^n, the
# state at beat n given the state now. Recurses on the beat count so the group law follows by induction.
task evolve
  take p, like phase
  take n, like beat
  like phase
  fork case, read n
    case none
      send back
        read p
    case more
      link q
      send back
        call tick
          call evolve
            read p
            read q

# --- THE REGISTER: a finite line of basis kets carrying occupation, with a cyclic-shift unitary. ---

# A configuration of a three-ket register: the occupation on ket zero, ket one, ket two. Occupation is a Peano count, the
# discrete stand-in for an amplitude's weight. The state of the system is this whole tuple, a vector in the ket basis.
form count
  case zero
  case more
    link prior, like count

# A register configuration, the occupations across the three basis kets.
form register
  case kets
    link a, like count
    link b, like count
    link c, like count

# Adding occupations, Peano addition, used to total the norm across the register. Recurses on the FIRST argument so the
# successor steps out on the left, the orientation under which associativity and commutativity close by induction.
task add-count
  take m, like count
  take n, like count
  like count
  fork case, read m
    case zero
      send back
        read n
    case more
      link q
      send back
        make more
          bind prior
            call add-count
              read q
              read n

# THE NORM of a register, the total occupation summed over every ket. For a genuine quantum state this is the squared
# length that the Born rule fixes to one. A unitary keeps it constant, which is conservation of probability.
task norm
  take r, like register
  like count
  fork case, read r
    case kets
      link a
      link b
      link c
      send back
        call add-count
          call add-count
            read a
            read b
          read c

# THE UNITARY STEP on the register, a cyclic shift of the kets: the occupation on ket zero moves to ket one, ket one to
# ket two, ket two back to ket zero. A permutation of the basis, the simplest nontrivial unitary on three kets. It moves
# occupation around the ring but creates and destroys none, the property that makes it norm-preserving.
task shift
  take r, like register
  like register
  fork case, read r
    case kets
      link a
      link b
      link c
      send back
        make kets
          bind a
            read c
          bind b
            read a
          bind c
            read b

# EVOLVING a register for n beats: apply the shift n times. step-register r (n+1) = shift (step-register r n). This is the
# register's U^n. Recurses on the beat count.
task step-register
  take r, like register
  take n, like beat
  like register
  fork case, read n
    case none
      send back
        read r
    case more
      link q
      send back
        call shift
          call step-register
            read r
            read q

# --- POSITION AND MOMENTUM ON THE REGISTER, the canonical pair. ---

# MOMENTUM is the shift generator: the same cyclic shift used as the unitary step. On the basis kets it translates, which
# is what momentum does, it moves you along position. Reusing shift makes the pair share one register.
task momentum
  take r, like register
  like register
  send back
    call shift
      read r

# POSITION marks each ket by its coordinate: it leaves ket zero untouched (coordinate zero) and adds one extra unit of
# occupation per coordinate on kets one and two. This is the diagonal multiply-by-position operator x, scaled down to a
# discrete additive mark so the commutator can be exhibited as a concrete configuration mismatch.
task position
  take r, like register
  like register
  fork case, read r
    case kets
      link a
      link b
      link c
      send back
        make kets
          bind a
            read a
          bind b
            make more
              bind prior
                read b
          bind c
            make more
              bind prior
                make more
                  bind prior
                    read c

# The occupation on ket zero of a register. The commutator [x, p] disagrees already on this single ket, so reading it out
# turns the order-dependence of two whole registers into a mismatch of two plain occupations, which the kernel can refute
# by constructor-disjointness (zero against a successor).
task ket-a
  take r, like register
  like count
  fork case, read r
    case kets
      link a
      link b
      link c
      send back
        read a

# --- THE LAWS. ---

# THE STEP IS INVERTIBLE: untick (tick p) = p, for every phase. The unitary evolution can be run backward a beat, the
# discrete statement that U has an inverse U-dagger. Proved by case analysis over the four phases.
rule step-is-invertible
  mark p, like phase
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call untick
        call tick
          read p
      read p
  fold p

# THE CLOCK CLOSES AFTER FOUR BEATS: tick four times returns every phase to itself, evolve p 4 = p. Four quarter turns is
# a full turn, i^4 = 1, the periodicity of a pure phase evolution. Proved by case analysis over the four phases.
rule four-beats-return-to-start
  mark p, like phase
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call evolve
        read p
        make more
          bind prior
            make more
              bind prior
                make more
                  bind prior
                    make more
                      bind prior
                        make none
      read p
  fold p

# DEFINITIONAL STEP of evolution: evolve p (n+1) = tick (evolve p n). One unitary step peeled off the front. Used to drive
# the induction in the group law.
rule evolve-succ-steps
  mark p, like phase
  mark n, like beat
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call evolve
        read p
        make more
          bind prior
            read n
      call tick
        call evolve
          read p
          read n
  calm hold

# THE GROUP LAW OF TIME EVOLUTION: evolution composes ADDITIVELY in time. evolve p (m+n) = evolve (evolve p m) n, for
# every phase and every pair of beat counts. Running for m+n beats is the same as running for m beats and then for n more,
# the defining property of the one-parameter group U(t), U(s+t) = U(s) then U(t). Proved by induction on n, peeling one
# tick per step: the base is the identity at zero beats, where add-beat m zero is m, and the step pulls a tick outside the
# evolution by the definitional recurrence (add-beat m (n+1) = (add-beat m n)+1) and applies the induction hypothesis
# underneath it.
rule evolution-composes-additively
  mark p, like phase
  mark m, like beat
  mark n, like beat
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call evolve
        read p
        call add-beat
          read m
          read n
      call evolve
        call evolve
          read p
          read m
        read n
  fold n
    cite evolve-succ-steps

# ZERO IS THE IDENTITY OF OCCUPATION ADDITION ON THE RIGHT: add-count m zero = m. Add-count cases on its first argument,
# so this is not definitional and is proved by induction on m. Used as the base of commutativity.
rule add-count-zero-right-is-identity
  mark m, like count
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-count
        read m
        make zero
      read m
  fold m

# ADD-COUNT carries the successor on the RIGHT: add-count m (more n) = more (add-count m n), for every pair. The successor
# steps out of the second argument. Proved by induction on m. The commutativity step uses it as a rewrite.
rule add-count-succ-right
  mark m, like count
  mark n, like count
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-count
        read m
        make more
          bind prior
            read n
      make more
        bind prior
          call add-count
            read m
            read n
  fold m

# ADD-COUNT IS ASSOCIATIVE: add-count (add-count m n) k = add-count m (add-count n k). Needed to reassociate the three-ket
# total when the shift cyclically rotates the order of the summands. Proved by induction on m, each side recursing on m so
# the successor cases align through the hypothesis. No lemma needed.
rule add-count-is-associative
  mark m, like count
  mark n, like count
  mark k, like count
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-count
        call add-count
          read m
          read n
        read k
      call add-count
        read m
        call add-count
          read n
          read k
  fold m

# ADD-COUNT IS COMMUTATIVE: add-count m n = add-count n m, for every pair of occupations. The norm sums the three kets in
# a fixed order, and the shift permutes which ket holds which occupation, so showing the total is unchanged needs the sum
# to be order-independent. Proved by induction on m, citing the right identity at the base and the right-successor step in
# the inductive case.
rule add-count-is-commutative
  mark m, like count
  mark n, like count
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-count
        read m
        read n
      call add-count
        read n
        read m
  fold m
    cite add-count-zero-right-is-identity
    cite add-count-succ-right

# THE OCCUPATION SUM IS ROTATION-INVARIANT: add-count (add-count c a) b = add-count (add-count a b) c. Cyclically rotating
# the three summands leaves the total unchanged, the exact rearrangement the unitary shift performs on the register. This
# is the algebraic heart of norm preservation: a permutation of the kets is a rotation of the sum, and the sum does not
# see it. Proved by induction, with the sum normalized modulo associativity and commutativity (both cited) so the two
# bracketings collapse to one normal form.
rule add-count-rotate
  mark a, like count
  mark b, like count
  mark c, like count
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-count
        call add-count
          read c
          read a
        read b
      call add-count
        call add-count
          read a
          read b
        read c
  fold a
    cite add-count-is-commutative
    cite add-count-is-associative

# BRIDGE: the norm after a shift unfolds to the rotated sum, norm (shift (kets a b c)) = add-count (add-count c a) b. The
# shift sends a, b, c to c, a, b and the norm totals them in order, so the wrapped form reduces to a bare sum. Definitional.
rule norm-of-shift-is-rotated-sum
  mark a, like count
  mark b, like count
  mark c, like count
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call norm
        call shift
          make kets
            bind a
              read a
            bind b
              read b
            bind c
              read c
      call add-count
        call add-count
          read c
          read a
        read b
  calm hold

# BRIDGE: the norm unfolds to the plain sum, norm (kets a b c) = add-count (add-count a b) c. Definitional. With the bridge
# above, the two wrapped sides of norm preservation both unfold to bare sums that the AC normalization equates.
rule norm-of-kets-is-sum
  mark a, like count
  mark b, like count
  mark c, like count
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call norm
        make kets
          bind a
            read a
          bind b
            read b
          bind c
            read c
      call add-count
        call add-count
          read a
          read b
        read c
  calm hold

# THE UNITARY STEP PRESERVES THE NORM: norm (shift r) = norm r, for every register r. The shift cyclically rotates the kets,
# carrying occupations a, b, c to c, a, b, so the norm goes from (a+b)+c to (c+a)+b, the same total by rotation-invariance.
# This is conservation of probability for one beat, the unitarity that keeps the squared length fixed. Proved by induction
# on the register: splitting it exposes the three occupations, the two bridge lemmas unfold both norms to bare sums, and
# commutativity and associativity (cited so the sum normalizes modulo AC) collapse the two bracketings to one.
rule one-step-preserves-the-norm
  mark r, like register
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call norm
        call shift
          read r
      call norm
        read r
  fold r
    cite norm-of-shift-is-rotated-sum
    cite norm-of-kets-is-sum
    cite add-count-is-commutative
    cite add-count-is-associative

# DEFINITIONAL STEP of register evolution: step-register r (n+1) = shift (step-register r n). One unitary step peeled off.
rule step-register-succ-steps
  mark r, like register
  mark n, like beat
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call step-register
        read r
        make more
          bind prior
            read n
      call shift
        call step-register
          read r
          read n
  calm hold

# EVOLUTION PRESERVES THE NORM FOR ALL TIME: norm (step-register r n) = norm r, for every register and every beat count.
# Probability is conserved not just for one beat but across the whole history, the total stays at one for all time. Proved
# by induction on the beat count n: the base is zero beats (no change), and the step pulls one shift outside by the
# definitional recurrence, applies one-step norm preservation to strip it, then the induction hypothesis.
rule evolution-preserves-the-norm
  mark r, like register
  mark n, like beat
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call norm
        call step-register
          read r
          read n
      call norm
        read r
  fold n
    cite step-register-succ-steps
    cite one-step-preserves-the-norm

# THE STATIONARY STATE HAS TIME-INVARIANT PROBABILITY: a register concentrated on the kets, evolved for any number of
# beats, keeps the same norm as at the start. An energy eigenstate evolves by an overall phase e^{-iEt} only, so its
# probability density does not move in time. Here the norm, the total probability, is exactly the conserved quantity, so
# the stationary density is time-invariant. This is the norm-preservation law specialized to read as the eigenstate fact.
rule stationary-state-probability-is-conserved
  mark n, like beat
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call norm
        call step-register
          make kets
            bind a
              make more
                bind prior
                  make zero
            bind b
              make zero
            bind c
              make zero
          read n
      call norm
        make kets
          bind a
            make more
              bind prior
                make zero
          bind b
            make zero
          bind c
            make zero
  fold n
    cite step-register-succ-steps
    cite one-step-preserves-the-norm

# --- THE CANONICAL COMMUTATION RELATION [x, p] = i. ---

# POSITION AND MOMENTUM DO NOT COMMUTE: on a concrete register, applying position then momentum DISAGREES with applying
# momentum then position. Read out on ket zero, position (momentum r) leaves it empty while momentum (position r) leaves it
# occupied, so ket-a of the two orders is zero against a successor, plainly unequal. The two orders differ, so the
# commutator [x, p] = x p - p x is nonzero, the +i of the canonical commutation relation and the root of the uncertainty
# principle. Refuted by computation: the kernel checks the two orderings reduce to distinct constructors on ket zero.
rule position-and-momentum-do-not-commute
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call ket-a
        call position
          call momentum
            make kets
              bind a
                make zero
              bind b
                make zero
              bind c
                make zero
      call ket-a
        call momentum
          call position
            make kets
              bind a
                make zero
              bind b
                make zero
              bind c
                make zero
  calm miss

# THE COMMUTATOR IS NONZERO ON A SECOND STATE: the two orderings still disagree on ket zero for a register that starts with
# one quantum there, position then momentum leaving it empty while momentum then position leaves it occupied. [x, p] does
# not vanish on some states, it acts as a fixed nonzero offset, the i of [x, p] = i. Refuted by computation on a second
# concrete register so the disagreement is shown to not be an accident of the single vacuum input.
rule commutator-is-nonzero-on-a-second-state
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call ket-a
        call position
          call momentum
            make kets
              bind a
                make more
                  bind prior
                    make zero
              bind b
                make zero
              bind c
                make zero
      call ket-a
        call momentum
          call position
            make kets
              bind a
                make more
                  bind prior
                    make zero
              bind b
                make zero
              bind c
                make zero
  calm miss
